		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3526&amp;p=28070#p28070"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>I now see a “Plus” version of the radio for sale with AES256 support:<br><br><a href="https://www.buytwowayradios.com/tytera-md-9600.html" class="postlink">https://www.buytwowayradios.com/tytera-md-9600.html</a></div></blockquote>This is mainly just marketing<br><br>The MD-9600 always had a GPS and Non-GPS version. The "Plus" version seems to come with GPS as standard<br><br>As far as we are aware there are no hardware changes to the "Plus" version of the UV380 or the MD9600 and the AES encryption function is most likely being done in the firmware, and the official "Plus" version of firmware is likely to run on the last 2 non-Plus hardware revisions<br><br>i.e The last 2 hardware revisions are labelled V4A and V5 but there does not seem to be any difference in the actual hardware<br><br>The OpenGD77 V5 MD9600 firmware works on both the boards labelled V4A and also V5 because they are the same hardware.<br><br>Also, there is some speculation that the Non-STM CPU is something to do with the AES encryption, but the Non-STM CPU actually , as far as we know, has less functionality than the official STM32 CPU.<br>i.e we know the non-STM chip has half the number of interrupt priorities; 8 instead of 16,  hence why we had to change the firmware for the UV380 and MD9600 to only use 8 interrupt priorities.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Thu Dec 14, 2023 5:00 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
